  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its status still shows \"pending\" because it hasn't run for the first time yet.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view has about 14,000 web requests, stamped from 4 Oct to 3 Dec 2026, so some of them are dated in the future. Each request records:\n- **Request details:** the time (`timestamp`), the response code, the bytes sent and the client IP.\n- **What was asked for:** the URL, the request, the file extension and the host.\n- **Visitor details:** source and destination country, browser, operating system and referrer.\n\nResponse codes break down like this:\n\n| Code | Requests | Average bytes |\n|---|---|---|\n| 200 | 12,832 | about 5.9 KB |\n| 404 | 801 | about 5 KB |\n| 503 | 441 | 0 |\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-8ee4ba40 Web logs overview\".** It has 7 panels and opens on the last 7 days:\n- Three headline numbers: total requests, unique visitors (by client IP) and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time, split by response code.\n- A pie chart of response codes.\n- A table of the top URLs, with request count and bytes for each.\n- A bar chart of the top source countries.\n\nLink: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-8ee4ba40-web-logs-overview-cb7b4275f9da\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-8ee4ba40 5xx spike\"** (id `14ad077e-840c-407a-8741-bef51a32045d`).\n- **Schedule:** it checks the `kibana_sample_data_logs` index every minute.\n- **Condition:** it counts requests with a response code from 500 to 599 over the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is above 10.\n- **Check:** I ran the same 5xx query against the index and it matched all 441 requests with code 503.\n- **No notifications:** the rule has no connector attached, so when it fires the alert only shows up in Kibana. If you want an email, Slack or similar notification, a connector needs to be added.",
